## What I Learned
This challenge demonstrates two important cryptography 
concepts.

First — Diffie-Hellman key exchange. DH is a method 
for two parties to establish a shared secret over an 
insecure channel without ever transmitting the secret 
itself. Each side has a private value (a and b) and 
a public value (A and B). Both sides independently 
compute the same shared key using modular exponentiation. 
The security relies entirely on both private values 
remaining secret.

Second — information disclosure breaks cryptography. 
It does not matter how mathematically strong an 
encryption scheme is if the keys are exposed. Here 
b was accidentally written to the output file. With 
b known anyone can recompute the shared key and 
decrypt any message encrypted with it. This is a 
real world mistake — hardcoded secrets, logged 
credentials, and accidentally committed keys are 
among the most common vulnerabilities found in 
production systems and security audits.


